{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb1b2rnaC5xiNYLsLGS8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I proved `compiler_correct`, and `lean Main.lean` now runs with no errors or warnings, so there's no `sorry` left.\n\n**What happened to the file:** my first edit attempt used a Python script, which crashed on a Unicode character after it had already opened `Main.lean` for writing. That emptied the file. I rebuilt it from the copy I'd read at the start. The original definitions are restored word for word, and the only change is the new proof. Since `Main.lean` was never committed to git, you might want to compare it against your own copy if you have one.\n\n**How the proof works:** a direct induction on `compiler_correct` doesn't go through, because compiling `add l r` runs `l`'s code with more instructions after it and a non-empty stack. So I first proved a more general helper lemma:\n\n```lean\ntheorem compile_run (e : Expr) : ∀ (code : List Instr) (s : List Nat),\n    run (compile e ++ code) s = run code (eval e :: s)\n```\n\nIn words: running `e`'s compiled code followed by any further `code` is the same as pushing `eval e` onto the stack and then running `code`.\n\n- **`lit` case:** both sides reduce to the same thing.\n- **`add` case:** regroup the appends, apply the induction hypotheses for `l` and then `r`, and the `plus` step finishes it.\n\n`compiler_correct` is the special case where `code` and `s` are both empty."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":955,"cache_read_input_tokens":9736,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":955},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fb5c3cfd-9ca0-4192-aa3b-89a8dff03777